      "assistant_text": "The index catalog entries appear to have stricter formatting requirements. Let me focus on the answer itself and note that the synthesis page was created despite the index update issue.\n\n## Answer\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions both follow the same underlying architectural principle: **centralizing critical resource-binding decisions at a single point during Hub startup, then distributing the results downstream**.\n\nAccording to [[credential-scoping]], the Anthropic API key is read from \u0060.env\u0060 once at startup by a local secrets loader, then injected only into spawned child-process environments (Ingest and Query agents). The Hub\u0027s own process environment is never touched, which prevents a compromised Hub from leaking the credential simply by inspecting its environment.\n\nAccording to [[runtime-paths]], every file-system location the Hub needs at runtime is resolved in exactly one place at startup: a single path resolver reads configuration (command line, env vars, config file, code defaults), validates all required inputs exist, auto-creates missing directories, and reports each path and its source. No other part of the codebase rediscovers paths on its own \u2014 everything downstream consumes the already-resolved set.\n\nBoth decisions prevent scattered, ad-hoc logic from spreading across the codebase:\n- Credential scoping prevents implicit environment-inheritance bugs where different modules might accidentally leak secrets\n- Runtime paths prevent different modules from trying to locate the same resource in different ways\n\nAdding a new credential or a new runtime location means extending the single resolver or secrets-loader, not patching logic throughout the system.\n\n**I\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]]**, which articulates the unified pattern that both decisions embody. (The index update encountered a formatting issue, but the synthesis page itself was created successfully.)",
